    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1038/s41558-025-02554-0",
    "abstract": "The Antarctic Ice Sheet is subject to amplifying feedbacks which can accelerate ice loss and lead to effectively irreversible retreat. We here analyse the distinct nature and risk of long-term ice loss for each individual drainage basin under different levels of warming. Depending on topographic and climatic conditions, we find that ice loss in some basins unfolds gradually with warming, whereas other basins are characterized by a critical threshold or tipping point beyond which large parts eventually disintegrate. A first threshold, potentially as low as 1-2 \u00b0C above pre-industrial levels, triggers the long-term collapse of ~40% of marine ice volume in West Antarctica. Marine-based sectors in East Antarctica, representing ~5 m of potential sea-level rise, are at risk of losing stability at 2-5 \u00b0C. Our results imply that the Antarctic Ice Sheet does not act as one single tipping element, but rather as several tipping systems interacting across drainage basins.",
    "field": "Antarctic ice sheet stability",
    "problem": "Viewing the Antarctic Ice Sheet as a single tipping element obscures the fact that its individual drainage basins have distinct critical thresholds and tipping dynamics due to varying topographic and climatic conditions, preventing a detailed risk assessment.",
    "method": "The authors use the Parallel Ice Sheet Model in long-term, quasi-static warming simulations to systematically analyze the equilibrium response of 18 individual Antarctic drainage basins and identify critical temperature thresholds beyond which large-scale, irreversible ice loss is triggered.",
    "task": "The task is to analyse the distinct nature and risk of long-term ice loss for each individual Antarctic drainage basin under different levels of warming to identify critical thresholds or tipping points beyond which large parts disintegrate.",
